Abstract

Isolation is not a mood. It is a physical boundary condition that transforms a human habitat into a Closed-Loop Cognitive System (CLCS) — a cyber-physical-social system where sensory input is artificially impoverished, social exit is impossible, and the one-way light delay to Earth (\Delta \approx 1.3 s) exceeds the human conversational repair time. In a colony where AI handles production, administration, and logistics, the human mind is subjected to simultaneous sensory deprivation and role redundancy. Psychological collapse becomes a system failure mode with lethal consequences, not a medical anecdote.

We develop the first formal theory of isolation systems psychology that treats mental states as coupled stochastic dynamical variables on a networked interaction graph with formally verified AI mediators. Four theorems are proved, providing the hard-science foundation for Profession 22.

Theorem 1 — Impossibility of Passive Stability (Social Entropy–Latency Threshold): We prove that if the combined effective environmental novelty (sensory entropy plus Earth-contact information, the latter decaying exponentially with light delay) falls below a critical bound H_{\text{min}}, and no active control is applied, then group cohesion decays exponentially and systemic psychological cascade failure occurs with probability 1 as t \to \infty, regardless of individual resilience.

Theorem 2 — Optimal Meaning Injection (The Social HJB): We formulate the allocation of limited psychological resources (VR immersion, private space, AI counseling bandwidth) as a constrained stochastic optimal control problem. We derive the associated Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman (HJB) equation and prove the existence and uniqueness of a viscosity solution, establishing that a mathematically optimal policy for "meaning distribution" exists and can be approached computationally.

Theorem 3 — AI-Mediated Conflict Dampening with Verifiable Transparency: We design a Switched Heuristic Policy where formally verified AI agents (specified in Probabilistic Computation Tree Logic, PCTL) act as declared "social heat sinks" in the interaction graph. We prove that this policy prevents conflict cascades with a bounded sub-optimality guarantee relative to the HJB optimum, while the transparency constraint — every intervention is declared, auditable, and revocable — is encoded in the PCTL specification itself, satisfying the mandates of the AI Behavioral Auditor (Profession 7) and the Operational Alignment Engineer (Profession 9).

Theorem 4 — Lexicographic Psychological Survival: Extending the lexicographic preference framework from interplanetary economics, we prove that when any agent’s psychological state \psi_i(t) falls below a critical viability threshold X_{\text{crit}}, standard colony utility maximization is suspended. The planner must allocate resources in a strictly survival-dominant manner, independent of cost, to restore \psi_i \ge X_{\text{crit}}. This formalizes the non-negotiable priority of psychological integrity over all other mission objectives.

The theorems dictate a three-layer intervention architecture:

1. Micro-layer: Personalized neuro-cognitive feedback and local co-regulation pods to counteract the constant negative psychological drift.
2. Meso-layer: Dynamic habitat reconfiguration (lighting, spatial zoning, VR allocation) managed by the switched heuristic to dampen interpersonal friction.
3. Macro-layer: The Collective Meaning Architecture — a scheduled, optimal sequence of shared rituals and narratives designed by the Human Meaning Designer (Profession 24) that injects the necessary high-entropy, unifying stimuli into the closed system.

This dissertation establishes Profession 22 not as a therapeutic afterthought, but as the formal engineering discipline of cognitive and social infrastructure in a latency-bounded, AI-saturated extraterrestrial colony. Without it, all other professions fail, because a mind that cannot co-regulate cannot maintain any other system.

