Chapter 1. Introduction: The Psychological Failure Mode of a Closed, AI-Run Colony

All prior space psychology collapses two distinct phenomena into a single "stress" variable. Sensory Deprivation — the lack of novel, Earth-normal environmental stimuli — and Social Compression — inescapable high-stakes proximity to a small, fixed group — are physically and psychologically orthogonal, yet they compound in a lunar habitat. When AI agents (Profession 3) autonomously handle production, administration, and logistics, the human transitions from operator to inhabitant. The open question of Profession 24 — "Why are humans here?" — becomes a causal force: role redundancy dissolves external goal structure, and the psychological state vector \psi_i(t) drifts downward.

The 1.3-second one-way light delay to Earth makes real-time empathic co-regulation impossible. Mission Control, structurally, cannot be a therapist. Physical evacuation takes days. The colony is a Closed-Loop Cognitive System (CLCS) — a system where the only viable psychological regulation is internal, proactive, and mathematically guaranteed.

Existing literature does not model these constraints together. This dissertation does, using the tools of stochastic optimal control, formal verification, and networked dynamical systems. We reframe psychological stability not as a reactive medical service but as a proactive optimal control problem: given that sensory entropy is artificially low and social friction is artificially high, what intervention topology guarantees cognitive stability without exhausting finite resources?

