Chapter 4. Theorem 2: Optimal Meaning Injection — The Social Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman Equation

The colony must allocate a finite budget of meaning-making resources to counteract the negative drift. This is a constrained stochastic optimal control problem.

Definition 4.1 (Cost Functional).
The planner chooses a control policy u(t) \in \mathcal{U} (resource allocation vector across agents) to minimize

J(u) = \mathbb{E}\left[ \int_0^T \left( \sum_{i=1}^N c_i(u_i(t)) + \gamma \operatorname{Var}(\Psi(t)) \right) dt + \Phi(\Psi(T)) \right],

subject to the networked SDE dynamics, the hard constraint \psi_i(t) \ge X_{\text{crit}} for all i,t, and \sum_i u_i(t) \le R_{\max}.

Theorem 4.1 (Existence and Uniqueness of Viscosity Solution for the Social HJB).
The value function V(\Psi, t) = \inf_u J(u) satisfies the Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman equation:

-\frac{\partial V}{\partial t} = \inf_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left\{ \mathcal{L}^u V(\Psi, t) + \sum_i c_i(u_i) + \gamma \operatorname{Var}(\Psi) \right\},

where \mathcal{L}^u is the infinitesimal generator of the controlled SDE. Under standard Lipschitz and growth conditions, and with the state constraint \Psi \ge X_{\text{crit}}, there exists a unique continuous viscosity solution. This solution defines the optimal trade-off between resource cost and psychological variance reduction.

Proof Strategy. Adapt the viscosity solution framework from stochastic hydrology control (reservoir sedimentation) to the social domain. The networked SDE’s drift and diffusion are Lipschitz, the cost is coercive, and the state constraint defines a closed set. The comparison principle holds, guaranteeing uniqueness. \blacksquare

