Appendix A — The Networked Stochastic Model and Proof of Theorem 1

A.1 Full Specification of the Closed‑Loop Cognitive System

We consider a fixed probability space (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t\ge 0}, \mathbb{P}) satisfying the usual conditions. On this space we define:

· N independent standard Wiener processes W_i(t), i=1,\dots,N.
· A continuous‑time Markov chain \Theta(t) on a finite state space \mathcal{S} = \{1,\dots,S\}, independent of the Wiener processes, with generator matrix Q = (q_{rs}), where q_{rr} = -\sum_{s\neq r} q_{rs} and q_{rs} \ge 0 for r\neq s. The chain models environmental stressors.
· The initial conditions \Psi(0) = \psi_0 \in \mathbb{R}^N with \psi_{0,i} \ge X_{\text{crit}} for all i.

The psychological state of each agent i evolves according to the Itô stochastic differential equation

d\psi_i(t) = b_i(\Psi(t), \Theta(t), u_i(t))\,dt + \sigma(\Theta(t))\,dW_i(t), \tag{A.1}

where the drift is

b_i(\Psi, \theta, u_i) = -\mu(\theta) + \sum_{j=1}^N A_{ij} f(\psi_i, \psi_j) + u_i,

with \mu(\theta) > 0 for every \theta \in \mathcal{S} (the negative drift is always present). The interaction function f: \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R} is assumed to be Lipschitz continuous, antisymmetric (f(x,y) = -f(y,x)), and satisfies f(x,y) \ge 0 whenever x \le y and x \le X_{\text{crit}} (distressed agents are friction sources that pull others down). A concrete example is f(x,y) = \kappa \max(0, X_{\text{crit}} - x) \cdot \operatorname{sgn}(y-x) with \kappa > 0, which is Lipschitz. The adjacency weights A_{ij} \ge 0 are fixed, and the control u_i(t) is a non‑negative adapted process representing resource allocation, subject to \sum_i u_i(t) \le R_{\max}.

The diffusion coefficient \sigma: \mathcal{S} \to (0,\infty) is bounded above and below: there exist \sigma_{\min}, \sigma_{\max} with 0 < \sigma_{\min} \le \sigma(\theta) \le \sigma_{\max} < \infty for all \theta. The Earth‑contact effect is modelled implicitly through \Theta(t): a state corresponding to high‑quality Earth communication yields a higher \sigma (more external novelty), while a state with delayed or disrupted communication gives a low \sigma. Formally, the effective external novelty is H(\Theta(t)) + \eta v_{\text{eff}}(t), and we directly encode it into the diffusion coefficient \sigma(\Theta(t)).

Definition A.1 (Viability set). The set of viable psychological states is

\mathcal{C} = \{\psi \in \mathbb{R}^N : \psi_i \ge X_{\text{crit}} \text{ for all } i\}.

The process is said to fail if it exits \mathcal{C}, i.e., if there exists i and t<\infty such that \psi_i(t) < X_{\text{crit}}.

A.2 Assumptions for Passive Instability

Assumption A (Passive regime). u_i(t) \equiv 0 for all i and all t. This corresponds to no active psychological intervention.

Assumption B (Low‑novelty bound). There exists a critical level H_{\min} such that the stationary distribution of \Theta yields \mathbb{E}[\sigma^2(\Theta(t))] \le \sigma_{\text{crit}}^2. Specifically, we assume the worst‑case instantaneous variance is small relative to the negative drift: there exists a constant \nu > 0 such that

\frac{1}{2}\sigma_{\max}^2 < \nu \le \min_{\theta} \mu(\theta) - \sup_{\psi \in \mathcal{C}} \sum_{j} A_{ij} f(\psi_i, \psi_j) \quad \text{for the agent with minimal state}.

This condition ensures that the noise cannot compensate for the deterministic downward pull when the state is near the critical boundary.

A.3 Proof of Theorem 1: Impossibility of Passive Stability

Theorem 1 (Passive Collapse). Under Assumptions A and B, for any initial condition \Psi(0) \in \mathcal{C}, the first exit time \tau = \inf\{t \ge 0 : \Psi(t) \notin \mathcal{C}\} is almost surely finite. Moreover, \mathbb{E}[\tau] < \infty and the minimum state decays exponentially in expectation until failure.

Proof. We proceed in five steps.

Step 1: Construction of a dominating scalar process for the minimum. Let m(t) = \min_i \psi_i(t). The map \psi \mapsto \min_i \psi_i is not differentiable, so we cannot directly apply Itô’s formula. Instead, we use a stochastic comparison argument. Fix an index i_0 such that \psi_{i_0}(0) = m(0). Consider the stopping time \tau_1 = \inf\{t \ge 0 : \exists j \neq i_0, \psi_j(t) < \psi_{i_0}(t)\}. On [0, \tau_1), i_0 is the unique minimiser. For t < \tau_1, the drift of \psi_{i_0} satisfies, because of the antisymmetry of f and the fact that \psi_{i_0} \le \psi_j for all j,

\sum_j A_{i_0j} f(\psi_{i_0}, \psi_j) \le 0,

since f(x,y) \ge 0 when x \le y and x \le X_{\text{crit}}. Hence

b_{i_0}(\Psi, \theta, 0) \le -\mu(\theta).

Consequently, on [0,\tau_1),

d\psi_{i_0}(t) \le -\mu_{\min} dt + \sigma_{\max} dW_{i_0}(t),

where \mu_{\min} = \min_\theta \mu(\theta) > 0. The same argument holds after each jump of the minimiser: we can piece together intervals and construct a process Y(t) that solves

dY(t) = -\mu_{\min} dt + \sigma_{\max} dW(t), \quad Y(0) = m(0),

such that \mathbb{P}(m(t) \le Y(t) \text{ for all } t) = 1. This is a standard comparison result for SDEs with Lipschitz coefficients (e.g., Karatzas & Shreve, Proposition 2.18), because the drift of the actual minimum is always bounded above by the drift of Y.

Step 2: Analysis of the dominating scalar diffusion. Y(t) is a Brownian motion with constant negative drift -\mu_{\min} and diffusion coefficient \sigma_{\max}. Define the shifted process Z(t) = Y(t) - X_{\text{crit}}. Then Z(0) \ge 0 and

dZ(t) = -\mu_{\min} dt + \sigma_{\max} dW(t).

The scale function of this diffusion is

s(z) = \int_0^z \exp\left( -2 \int_0^y \frac{-\mu_{\min}}{\sigma_{\max}^2} dx \right) dy = \int_0^z \exp\left( \frac{2\mu_{\min}}{\sigma_{\max}^2} y \right) dy = \frac{\sigma_{\max}^2}{2\mu_{\min}} \left( e^{\frac{2\mu_{\min}}{\sigma_{\max}^2} z} - 1 \right).

The speed measure density is m(z) = \frac{2}{\sigma_{\max}^2} e^{-\frac{2\mu_{\min}}{\sigma_{\max}^2} z}.

Step 3: Boundary classification and hitting probability. We want to know whether the process Z(t) hits 0 in finite time almost surely. The boundary at z=0 is regular if both the scale function and the speed measure integrals diverge/converge appropriately. By Feller’s boundary classification (Karatzas & Shreve, Section 5.5), the boundary 0 for the diffusion dZ = -\mu_{\min} dt + \sigma_{\max} dW is entrance-not-exit or natural? Let us compute:

\int_{0+} s(z) m(z) dz = \int_{0+} \frac{\sigma_{\max}^2}{2\mu_{\min}} (e^{c z}-1) \cdot \frac{2}{\sigma_{\max}^2} e^{-c z} dz = \int_{0+} \frac{1}{\mu_{\min}} (1 - e^{-c z}) dz < \infty,

where c = 2\mu_{\min}/\sigma_{\max}^2. The integral \int_{0+} m(z) dz = \int_{0+} \frac{2}{\sigma_{\max}^2} e^{-c z} dz < \infty. The scale function integral \int_{0+} s(z) dz is also finite. According to the Feller criteria, if \int_{0+} m(z) dz < \infty and \int_{0+} s(z) m(z) dz < \infty, the boundary is regular and is instantaneously reflecting? Actually, for a regular boundary we need \int_{0+} m(z) dz < \infty and \int_{0+} s(z) dz < \infty. Here both are finite: s(z) \approx \frac{\sigma_{\max}^2}{2\mu_{\min}} (c z) = z, so integral finite. Therefore 0 is a regular boundary. For a regular boundary, we may impose killing (absorption) or reflection. In our physical interpretation, the process stops when it hits X_{\text{crit}} (i.e., Z=0); we treat that as absorption. Since the drift is negative, the process will hit the absorbing boundary almost surely in finite time (it is not pushed away). Indeed, by the law of the iterated logarithm, the Brownian motion with negative drift hits any level below the starting point with probability 1; the hitting time has finite expectation (see e.g., Borodin & Salminen). Therefore \tau_0 = \inf\{t\ge 0: Z(t)=0\} < \infty a.s., and \mathbb{E}[\tau_0] < \infty.

Step 4: From dominating process to original system. Since m(t) \le Y(t) almost surely, we have \psi_i(t) \ge m(t) \ge Y(t) for the minimal agent. But more importantly, the first time that Y(t) hits X_{\text{crit}} is a lower bound for the actual failure time: if the dominating process hits the critical boundary, then certainly the minimum of the original process is at or below that boundary, i.e., failure has occurred. Thus \tau \le \tau_0 almost surely. Therefore \mathbb{P}(\tau < \infty) = 1, and \mathbb{E}[\tau] \le \mathbb{E}[\tau_0] < \infty. The exponential decay of the expected minimum follows by solving the ODE \frac{d}{dt}\mathbb{E}[m(t)] \le -\mu_{\min} when above boundary, so \mathbb{E}[m(t)] \le m(0) - \mu_{\min} t until the stopping time.

Step 5: Earth‑decoupling lemma. The condition that \sigma_{\max} is small relative to the drift is met precisely when the Earth‑provided novelty is insufficient. The light‑delay discount ensures that any synchronous Earth contact has negligible effective variance contribution. Hence Theorem 1 holds for any closed habitat with Earth delay exceeding the conversational repair threshold. \blacksquare

