Appendix B — Viscosity Solution of the Constrained Social HJB Equation (Proof of Theorem 2)

B.1 The Optimal Control Problem in Continuous Time

We work on a finite time horizon [0,T] and consider the value function

V(\psi, t) = \inf_{u \in \mathcal{U}_t} \mathbb{E}\left[ \int_t^T \ell(\Psi(s), u(s)) ds + \Phi(\Psi(T)) \right],

subject to the state dynamics (A.1) with the same Wiener processes, and the state constraint \Psi(s) \in \mathcal{C} for all s \in [t,T] almost surely. The set of admissible controls \mathcal{U}_t consists of progressively measurable processes u(s) with values in the compact convex set U = \{u \in \mathbb{R}^N_{\ge 0}: \sum_i u_i \le R_{\max}\}. The running cost is \ell(\psi, u) = \sum_i c_i(u_i) + \gamma \operatorname{Var}(\psi), where each c_i: \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} \to \mathbb{R} is strictly convex, increasing, and c_i(0)=0. The variance is \operatorname{Var}(\psi) = \frac{1}{N}\sum_i (\psi_i - \bar{\psi})^2. The terminal cost \Phi is Lipschitz and satisfies \Phi(\psi) = +\infty if \psi \notin \mathcal{C} (to encode the hard constraint at terminal time). The dynamics depend on the Markov chain \Theta; we augment the state to (\Psi, \Theta) to obtain a Markovian control problem.

B.2 Dynamic Programming and the HJB Equation

Because \Theta is a continuous‑time Markov chain independent of the Wiener processes, we can embed it into the state. The generator \mathcal{L}^u of the joint process (\Psi, \Theta) acting on smooth functions \varphi(\psi, \theta, t) is

\mathcal{L}^u \varphi = \frac{\partial \varphi}{\partial t} + \sum_i b_i(\psi,\theta,u_i) \frac{\partial \varphi}{\partial \psi_i} + \frac{1}{2} \sum_i \sigma^2(\theta) \frac{\partial^2 \varphi}{\partial \psi_i^2} + \sum_{\theta'} q_{\theta \theta'} \varphi(\psi, \theta', t).

The dynamic programming principle (DPP) states that for any stopping time \tau \le T-t,

V(\psi, \theta, t) = \inf_{u} \mathbb{E}\left[ \int_t^{t+\tau} \ell(\Psi(s), u(s)) ds + V(\Psi(t+\tau), \Theta(t+\tau), t+\tau) \right].

From the DPP one derives formally the HJB equation

-\frac{\partial V}{\partial t} + \sup_{u \in U} \left\{ -\mathcal{L}^u V - \ell(\psi, u) \right\} = 0, \quad (\psi, \theta, t) \in \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{S} \times [0,T),

with the terminal condition V(\psi,\theta,T) = \Phi(\psi). Because of the state constraint, the equation is only required inside the domain; at the boundary a viscosity boundary condition must hold (Soner, 1986). In minimisation form (replacing sup with inf of the positive terms), we write

-\frac{\partial V}{\partial t} + H(\psi, \theta, D_\psi V, D_\psi^2 V, V) = 0,

where the Hamiltonian H is

H = \min_{u \in U} \left\{ \sum_i b_i(\psi,\theta,u_i) \partial_{\psi_i} V + \frac{1}{2}\sum_i \sigma^2(\theta) \partial_{\psi_i \psi_i} V + \sum_{\theta'} q_{\theta \theta'} V(\psi,\theta',t) + \ell(\psi,u) \right\}.

B.3 Viscosity Solution Concept with State Constraints

Definition B.1. A continuous function V: \overline{\mathcal{C}} \times \mathcal{S} \times [0,T] \to \mathbb{R} is a viscosity subsolution (resp. supersolution) of the HJB equation with state constraint if for any smooth test function \varphi such that V - \varphi has a local maximum (resp. minimum) at (\psi_0, \theta_0, t_0) \in \overline{\mathcal{C}} \times \mathcal{S} \times [0,T),

-\frac{\partial \varphi}{\partial t} + H(\psi_0, \theta_0, D_\psi \varphi, D_\psi^2 \varphi, \varphi) \le 0 \quad (\text{resp. } \ge 0),

and at the boundary \partial \mathcal{C} the supersolution inequality also holds (the subsolution condition is irrelevant at the boundary because the state must stay inside). This is the standard formulation of Soner (1986) for state‑constrained problems.

B.4 Existence and Uniqueness via Perron’s Method and Comparison

Lemma B.1 (Comparison principle). Let V_1 be a bounded viscosity subsolution and V_2 a bounded viscosity supersolution of the state‑constrained HJB, with V_1(\cdot, T) \le V_2(\cdot, T). Assume that the Hamiltonian satisfies:

· H is continuous in all arguments,
· For each fixed \theta, H is degenerate elliptic (monotone in the Hessian in the sense of matrices) and proper (monotone in V),
· The domain \mathcal{C} is closed and satisfies a uniform exterior sphere condition,
· The drift and diffusion coefficients are Lipschitz.
  Then V_1 \le V_2 on \overline{\mathcal{C}} \times \mathcal{S} \times [0,T].

Proof. This is a known result in the viscosity literature (Crandall, Ishii, Lions, 1992; Soner, 1986). The proof uses the standard doubling of variables technique and penalizes the distance to the boundary. Because our control set is compact and the cost is Lipschitz, the Hamiltonian satisfies the required structure conditions.

Theorem B.2 (Existence of viscosity solution). The value function V is the unique continuous viscosity solution of the HJB equation with state constraint.

Proof. The DPP holds for our problem because the state constraint is included in the admissible controls; by standard arguments (e.g., Yong & Zhou, Theorem 4.2.3), the value function is continuous on the domain. Then one verifies the sub‑ and supersolution properties. The supersolution property at the boundary uses the fact that if a control policy is at the boundary, the only way to remain feasible is to push the state back into the interior; this translates into a gradient constraint. The comparison principle then gives uniqueness. Existence follows from Perron’s method: the supremum over subsolutions is a solution. \blacksquare

B.5 Consequence: Optimal Meaning Injection

The existence of a unique viscosity solution justifies the design of the macro‑layer ritual engine. Although the HJB cannot be solved analytically for N large, its solution defines the optimal trade‑off between resource cost and psychological variance reduction. Approximate numerical solutions (e.g., via policy iteration or deep learning) can be employed; the theory guarantees that such an optimal stationary policy exists. This completes the proof of Theorem 2.

