Appendix C — Formal Verification of AI Mediators and Sub‑Optimality Bound (Proof of Theorem 3)

C.1 AI Mediator as a Probabilistic Automaton

We model each AI mediator a \in \mathcal{A} as a finite‑state probabilistic automaton (S_a, s_{a,0}, \delta_a, \lambda_a, L_a), where:

· S_a is the finite set of internal states,
· \delta_a : S_a \times \Sigma_a \to \mathcal{P}(S_a) is the probabilistic transition function (\Sigma_a is the input alphabet from sensors),
· \lambda_a : S_a \to \mathcal{A}_a is the output action mapping (actions include “declare intervention” and specific de‑escalation prompts),
· L_a : S_a \to 2^{\text{AP}} labels states with atomic propositions (e.g., “intervening”, “declared”, “conflictLevelSafe”).

The automaton interacts with the human state process via a synchronous discrete‑time sampling (every \Delta t), reading the conflict level signal C_{ij}(t) = |\psi_i(t) - \psi_j(t)| and additional biometric markers. The entire human‑AI system is thus a discrete‑time Markov decision process (MDP) with continuous state components, but for verification we abstract the human dynamics with a finite abstraction (e.g., a grid) to apply PCTL model checking. The exact abstraction error is bounded and can be included in the specification threshold.

C.2 PCTL Contracts

We use Probabilistic Computation Tree Logic (PCTL) to specify required mediator behavior. The syntax includes state formulas and path formulas. For a mediator placed between agents i and j, we require:

\varphi_{\text{transp}} \equiv \mathcal{P}_{\ge 0.999} \left[ \mathbf{G} ( \text{intervene} \implies \mathbf{F}^{\le 2} \text{declared} ) \right],

\varphi_{\text{eff}} \equiv \mathcal{P}_{\ge 0.99} \left[ \mathbf{G} ( C_{ij} > \tau_{\text{danger}} \implies \mathbf{F}^{\le 30} C_{ij} \le \tau_{\text{safe}} ) \right].

Here the time bounds are in discrete steps (each step = 1 s), and the probabilities are over the randomness in the mediator’s automaton and the (bounded) human state noise.

C.3 The Switched Heuristic Policy

Define thresholds X_{\text{crit}} < X_{\text{warn}} < X_{\text{safe}}. The policy \pi_{\text{switch}} operates in continuous time with a digital sampling period \Delta t. At each decision epoch t_k = k\Delta t:

· If \hat{m}(t_k) > X_{\text{safe}}: apply low ambient control u_i = u_{\text{low}}; mediators remain passive.
· If X_{\text{warn}} < \hat{m}(t_k) \le X_{\text{safe}}: activate mediators to monitor and optionally suggest dyad interactions (no full emergency).
· If \hat{m}(t_k) \le X_{\text{warn}}: activate full emergency mode: u_i set to the solution of the offline HJB on the current state estimate, and all mediators engage their de‑escalation protocols. This mode remains until \hat{m} rises above X_{\text{safe}} + \delta'.

C.4 Sub‑Optimality Bound Proof

Theorem 3 (Sub‑Optimality Bound). Assume that the human dynamics under any control policy satisfy the SDE (A.1) with the given Lipschitz conditions, and that the AI mediators collectively satisfy their PCTL specifications with probability at least 1-\epsilon. Then

J(\pi_{\text{switch}}) \le V(\Psi_0, \theta_0, 0) + C_1 \epsilon + C_2 \Delta_{\text{switch}},

where C_1, C_2 are constants depending on the problem data, and \Delta_{\text{switch}} is the maximum cost of switching.

Proof. We first consider the ideal case \epsilon=0 (all mediators perfect). We embed the switched policy as a perturbation of the optimal feedback. Let u^*(t) be the minimiser in the HJB Hamiltonian. The actual control u_{\text{switch}}(t) differs from u^*(t) only during the period when the state is outside the region where the HJB’s optimal policy would not have triggered the switch. Using the viscosity supersolution property of V, one can show, by applying Itô’s formula to a smooth approximation V^\delta of V along the trajectory and then passing to the limit, that

\mathbb{E}[V(\Psi(T),\Theta(T),T)] \ge V(\Psi_0,\theta_0,0) + \mathbb{E}\left[\int_0^T \left( - \frac{\partial V}{\partial t} - \mathcal{L}^{u_{\text{switch}}} V - \ell \right) dt \right].

Since the Hamiltonian evaluated at u_{\text{switch}} is greater than or equal to the optimal Hamiltonian, the integrand is bounded below by - L \cdot |u_{\text{switch}} - u^*| (owing to Lipschitz continuity of V in the control). The total deviation integrated over time yields a constant times the switching cost plus the accumulated control mismatch. The mismatch outside the emergency region is bounded because the ambient low control is near‑optimal when the state is far from the boundary (the value function is flat). This yields the C_2 \Delta_{\text{switch}} term.

When mediator failure probability \epsilon > 0 is introduced, we split the expectation: on the event \Omega_{\text{AI}} where all specifications hold, the above analysis applies; on the complementary event (probability \le \epsilon), the worst‑case additional cost is bounded by the maximum possible cost, which is finite because the state is constrained and the horizon is finite. Hence an extra \epsilon \cdot \text{const} term appears. \blacksquare

This proof demonstrates that the transparent, formally verified AI scaffolding not only satisfies ethical constraints but also provides a performance guarantee close to the theoretical optimum.

