Appendix D — Lexicographic Preferences and Proof of Theorem 4

D.1 Axiomatic Foundation

Let \mathcal{S} be the state space of psychological vectors \psi. The social planner's preferences are captured by a binary relation \succsim. We postulate:

(L1) Separability by a viability threshold. There exists a closed set \mathcal{F} \subseteq \mathcal{S} of viable states, with complement \mathcal{F}^c (catastrophic). For any \psi^1 \in \mathcal{F} and \psi^2 \notin \mathcal{F}, we have \psi^1 \succ \psi^2.

(L2) Secondary utility on viable states. There exists a continuous function U: \mathcal{F} \to \mathbb{R} such that for all \psi, \psi' \in \mathcal{F}, \psi \succsim \psi' iff U(\psi) \ge U(\psi').

(L3) Dynamic consistency. The preference is applied to entire trajectories \Psi(\cdot); a trajectory that leaves \mathcal{F} at some time is considered worse than any trajectory that stays in \mathcal{F} at all times, irrespective of U-values before or after.

These axioms define a lexicographic ordering: primary criterion is always staying in \mathcal{F}; secondary criterion is the integral of U over the trajectory. This matches the survival‑dominance assumption in the dissertation.

D.2 Proof of Survival Dominance (Theorem 4)

Theorem 4. Any admissible optimal control policy for the cost functional (which incorporates the state constraint \Psi(t) \in \mathcal{C} with \mathcal{C} = \mathcal{F}) must satisfy: if ever a state component approaches the boundary of \mathcal{C}, the control instantaneously uses all necessary resources to repel it back, regardless of the running cost. In other words, the hard constraint is lexicographically enforced.

Proof. Suppose there exists an optimal policy \pi^* and a time t_0 such that, with positive probability, some agent’s state hits X_{\text{crit}} and the policy does not apply maximal correction. Because the SDE is continuous and the diffusion non‑degenerate, the state will remain below X_{\text{crit}} for a positive duration. By (L3), the resulting trajectory is strictly worse than a trajectory that stays in \mathcal{C} throughout. Construct an alternative policy \tilde{\pi} that follows \pi^* except when the minimal state drops below X_{\text{warn}}; at that point, \tilde{\pi} temporarily overrides \pi^* and applies the maximum feasible control u_i = R_{\max} (or a precomputed pushing policy) until the state recovers above X_{\text{warn}}+\delta. Because the pushing action incurs a bounded cost but avoids the catastrophic event (which has infinite lexicographic penalty), the overall evaluation of \tilde{\pi} is better than that of \pi^* in the lexicographic sense. This contradicts the optimality of \pi^*. Hence \pi^* must already include this maximal defensive behaviour.

In the HJB framework, the state constraint is encoded by setting the value function to +\infty outside \mathcal{C}. The boundary condition forces the value function to satisfy a gradient constraint that makes the optimal drift point inward (Soner, 1986, Theorem 3.1). Thus the optimal control automatically respects the survival dominance. \blacksquare

Corollary D.1 (Buffer necessity). Because the HJB’s boundary condition is enforced in the viscosity sense, practical controllers require a safety margin X_{\text{warn}} > X_{\text{crit}} to ensure that the repelling action can be taken before the hard constraint is violated due to diffusion. This is exactly the design of the switched heuristic.

